724 0 obj
  << /ActualText (The Boston Housing dataset is derived from the information collected by the U\056S\056 Census Service concerning housing in the area of Boston in 1970\056 It consists of a total of 506 samples\054 each containing 12 numeric features that explains one numeric target variable\056 The dataset has no missing values\056)
     /K [ 1 ]
     /P 725 0 R
     /Pg 722 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

725 0 obj
  << /K [ 724 0 R ]
     /P 742 0 R
     /S /P
     /Pg 722 0 R
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

726 0 obj
  << /ActualText (We designed a predictive pipeline to model Boston housing prices \050MEDV\051 by benchmarking three classical machine learning algorithms against the simplest deep learning architecture\054 a multilayer perceptron\056\n)
     /K [ 2 ]
     /P 727 0 R
     /Pg 722 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

727 0 obj
  << /K [ 726 0 R ]
     /P 742 0 R
     /S /P
     /Pg 722 0 R
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

728 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Data preprocessing \046 validation split\nData normalization\056 The columns CRIM\054 ZN\054 AGE\054 DIS\054 RAD\054 TAX and LSTAT were standard scaled \050mean of 0 and standard deviation of 1\051 with Z normalization to keep a similar magnitude between the features\056\nValidation strategy\056 To guarantee fair benchmarking\054 all models were trained and tested on the same train\055test ratio\054 as well as the same random state for the splitting of the dataset\056 Furthermore\054 a strict 5\055fold Cross Validation strategy was enforced across all models\054 ensuring identical evaluation subsets\056)
     /K [ 3 ]
     /P 729 0 R
     /Pg 722 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

729 0 obj
  << /K [ 728 0 R ]
     /P 742 0 R
     /S /P
     /Pg 722 0 R
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

730 0 obj
  << /ActualText (K\055Fold Cross Validation \046 Hyperparameter tuning\nK\055fold cross validation \050CV\051 was used to evaluate the robustness of the models and estimate their predictive performance\056 For the classical ML algorithms\054 it was used to determine the optimal model and the corresponding hyperparameters that bring the best results\056 For the neural network\054 CV was used to find the hyperparameters that offer a good tradeoff between generalization and performance\056 Afterwards\054 the selected hyperparameters were used to train with the complete training set for the final model\056\n)
     /K [ 4 ]
     /P 731 0 R
     /Pg 722 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

731 0 obj
  << /K [ 730 0 R ]
     /P 742 0 R
     /S /P
     /Pg 722 0 R
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

732 0 obj
  << /ActualText (The models were evaluated across three regression performance metrics\056 The tree ensemble methods outperformed both SVR and the deep learning approach\054 with XGBoost achieving the highest overall prediction accuracy\056)
     /K [ 5 ]
     /P 733 0 R
     /Pg 722 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

733 0 obj
  << /K [ 732 0 R ]
     /P 742 0 R
     /S /P
     /Pg 722 0 R
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

734 0 obj
  << /ActualText (The Actual vs\056 Predicted plot shows clusters tightly aligned with the identity line\054 proving a high prediction accuracy along the dataset\056)
     /K [ 6 ]
     /P 735 0 R
     /Pg 722 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

735 0 obj
  << /K [ 734 0 R ]
     /P 742 0 R
     /S /P
     /Pg 722 0 R
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

736 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Random Forest and XGBoost show a tight\054 symmetric Gaussian bell centered at 0\054 meaning they have consistent error variance across all values\056 Meanwhile\054 MLP and SVR show wider\054 more distributed tails\054 meaning a few severe outliers in the predictions\056)
     /K [ 7 ]
     /P 737 0 R
     /Pg 722 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

